  "text": "umably adjacent. This leads to an equilateral triangle where the height \\(h\\) is given by \\(\\sqrt{3}/{2}\\) times the side length \\(s\\). However, we are given a diameter of 14 cm, so we plug in the value of \\(d\\) plus \\(\\sqrt{2}\\) and the equation to get:\n\n\\[d_{\\text{new}}^2 = \\frac{3\\sqrt{3}}{2} s^2\\]\n\nSolving for \\(d_{\\text{new}}\\) gives us the actual height of the triangle after adjustments. The smallest possible area of \\(\\triangle PQR\\) would then be:\n\n\\[A_{PQR} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times b \\times h\\]\n\nWe need to determine the smallest possible side length of the triangle given that \\(b=b_{\\text{new}} + \\sqrt{2}\\), and don't assume anything else yet. We find from the above that the sidelength can be calculated by substituting \\(d\\) into \\(d_{\\text{new}}^2 = \\sqrt{3} s^2\\):\n\n\\[14^2 = 3 \\sqrt{3} \\times s^2\\]\n\nSolving for \\(s\\):\n\n\\[s = \\frac{14}{\\sqrt{3 \\sqrt{3}}}\\]\n\n\\[s = \\frac{14}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\frac{1}{\\sqrt[4]{3}}\\]\n\n\\[s = \\frac{14}{\\sqrt{3}} \\times \\frac{1}{3^{1/4}}\\]\n\nWe see that \\( \\sqrt{3} \\approx 1.732 \\):\n\n\\[s \\approx \\frac{14}{1.732}\\]\n\n\\[s \\approx 8.077\\]\n\nThe smallest possible area of \\(\\triangle PQR\\) is therefore:\n\n\\[A_{PQR} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times (8.077 + \\sqrt{2}) \\times \\frac{14}{\\sqrt{3}}\\]\n\nPlugging in the numbers:\n\n\\[A_{PQR} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8.077 + \\sqrt{2} \\times \\frac{14}{\\sqrt{3}}\\]\n\n\\[A_{PQR} = \\frac{1}{2} \\times 8.077 + \\sqrt{2} \\times \\frac{14}{1.732}\\]\n\n\\[A_{PQR} = 4.0385 + 5.1149\\]\n\n\\[A_{PQR} \\approx 9.1534\\]\n\nTherefore, the smallest possible area of \\(\\triangle PQR\\) given the restrictions is approximately 9.1534 square cms.",
